---
/* THE BLADE — 04/10/2026, Session 28. The rocker figure is BladeRocker, drawn from
   rig-math.js; the numbers in the prose are computed from the same constants, so the
   page cannot say one thing while the skaters on every other page stand on another.
   Facts not in the rig (7 and 8 ft rockers, the common hollows, a 1/2 in default) are
   from a skating club's equipment guide, cited at the foot of the page. */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import BladeRocker from '../../components/BladeRocker.astro';
import BladeHollow from '../../components/BladeHollow.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url.js';
import { kitPage } from '../../lib/kit.js';
import { ROCKER, MAX_BLADE_PITCH, contactAlong } from '../../lib/rig-math.js';
const perDegree = contactAlong(1).toFixed(1);
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Kit', href: url('kit/') }, { name: 'The blade' }];
---
<Base title="The blade" subtitle={kitPage('blade').summary} crumbs={crumbs}>
  <div class="crumbs" role="navigation" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><p class="lede"><a href={url('kit/')}>Kit</a></p></div>
  <h1>The blade</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A figure blade is not flat along its length and not flat across it. Both curves decide
    how it skates.
  </p>

  <h2>The rocker</h2>
  <p>
    Along its length the blade is ground to a curve called the rocker. Figure blades are
    commonly ground to a radius of about 7 or 8 feet; the figures in this guide stand on
    one of {Math.round(ROCKER / 30.48)} feet. A tighter rocker turns more easily and a flatter one runs
    faster and steadier. Many blades are also ground a little tighter towards the front,
    the part a spin sits on.
  </p>
  <BladeRocker />
  <p>
    Because the blade is curved, only a short stretch of it touches the ice at once, and
    which stretch depends on how the boot is tilted. Tilting the foot forward by one degree
    moves the contact {perDegree} cm towards the toe. Past about {MAX_BLADE_PITCH.toFixed(1)} degrees
    the contact runs off the front of the blade altogether, and the next thing to touch the
    ice is the pick.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why a coach talks so much about where your weight is. Gliding and most edges
    sit a little behind the middle. Spins sit forward, close to the pick. Rocking too far
    back puts a skater on the heel, where the blade stops steering.
  </p>

  <h2>The toe pick</h2>
  <p>
    The teeth at the front are the toe pick. They are for jabbing into the ice: the vault
    of a toe loop, flip or Lutz, and the push of a toe-assisted hop. They are not for
    standing on, and a skater who drifts forward onto them while gliding trips. The pick
    is also why figure skates are sharpened differently from hockey skates, which have
    none.
  </p>

  <h2>The hollow and the two edges</h2>
  <p>
    Across its width the blade is sharpened with a shallow groove, the hollow, which leaves
    a sharp edge on each side: the inside edge and the outside edge that every element in
    the guide is written in. The groove is part of a circle, and it is named by that
    circle's radius. A smaller radius cuts a deeper groove, grips harder and is harder to
    stop on; a larger one is shallower and slides more.
  </p>
  <BladeHollow />
  <p>
    Most figure skaters skate on a hollow between 7/16 and 5/8 of an inch, and 1/2 inch is
    the usual starting point. A sharpener can suggest a different one for your weight and
    what you skate. It is a small number with a large effect, so change it one step at a
    time.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to next</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><a href={url('kit/care/')}>Looking after skates</a><span>Guards, soakers, drying and sharpening.</span></li>
    <li><a href={url('elements/in/edges/')}>The eight edges</a><span>The inside and outside edges, on each foot, both directions.</span></li>
    <li><a href={url('elements/in/spin/')}>Spins</a><span>The elements that live at the front of the blade.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">
    Rocker radii and common hollows: Charleston Figure Skating Club,{' '}
    <a href="https://charlestonfsc.com/next-steps/equipment/blades/">Equipment: blades</a>.
    Blade width: Encyclopaedia Britannica,{' '}
    <a href="https://www.britannica.com/sports/blade-ice-skating">Blade</a>. Makers publish
    the rocker and the hollow of each blade but, on the pages checked, not its width.
    Everything else on this page is computed from the blade the guide's figures stand on.
  </p>
</Base>
